"use strict";(self.webpackChunkmy_site=self.webpackChunkmy_site||[]).push([["7358"],{1895(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1","source":"@site/blog/Differential Geometry and Fibre Bundle/1_1.mdx","title":"Basic Topology","description":"It\'s assumed that you have basic knowledge of sets, including the definition, Cartesian product, etc.","date":"2026-05-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Differential Geometry","permalink":"/blog/tags/differential-geometry"},{"inline":true,"label":"Topology","permalink":"/blog/tags/topology"}],"readingTime":4.87,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Basic Topology","date":"2026-05-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["Differential Geometry","Topology"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"> It\'s assumed that you have basic knowledge of sets, including the definition, Cartesian product, etc.\\n>\\n> In fact, the majority of differential geometry does not require much of the knowledge of topology, at least not the basic definitions. It\'s suggested that beginners should skip this chapter and read the followings directly.\\n\\n# Definition: Topology\\n\\nWhen mathematicians and physicists refer to \\"topology\\", there are some subtle differences involved. Here we focus on the rigorous definition of topology:\\n\\n:::tip[Topology]\\n\\nA set $\\\\mathscr{T}$ is called the **topology** of a set $X$, if $\\\\mathscr{T}$ contains all of the \\"open subsets\\" of $X$. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Conditions for topology]\\n\\nTo assure the definition is self-consistent (**all** of the open subsets are contained), $\\\\mathscr{T}$ should satisfy the following conditions:\\n \\n1. $X, \\\\varnothing \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$;\\n2. if $\\\\ O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T},  i = 1,  2,  \\\\dots,  n$, then $\\\\bigcap\\\\limits_{i=1}^n O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$;\\n3. if$\\\\ O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T},  \\\\forall i$, then $\\\\bigcup\\\\limits_{i} O_i \\\\in \\\\mathscr{T}$. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note\\n\\nNotice that condition 2 require $n$ to be a finite integer, while condition 3 does not. This means only finite intersections remains open, while the union of open sets remains open, even if we take infinite sets. For example, consider: $U_n = \\\\{x | \\\\left|x\\\\right| < 1+ \\\\frac{1}{n} \\\\}$, then $\\\\bigcap\\\\limits_{i=1}^\\\\infty U_n \\\\to [-1,  1]$, not a open set of $\\\\mathbb{R}$!\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Examples of topology\\n\\nHere we offer three examples of different types of topology. In practice for a given set $X$, there are usually infinitely many ways to specify a topology.\\n\\n:::info[Usual topology]\\n\\nSuppose $X = \\\\mathbb{R}^n$, we say that $\\\\mathscr{T}_u = \\\\{\\\\varnothing, \\\\bigcup_{x_0, r} B(x_0, r)\\\\}$ is the **usual topology** of $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$, where $B(x_0, r) = \\\\{x \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^n| \\\\left|x-x_0\\\\right| < r\\\\}$ for $x_0 \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}^n, r>0 \\\\in \\\\mathbb{R}$ is called the open ball. \\n\\nNotice that the distance in $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$ is defined as: $\\\\left|x-x_0\\\\right| = \\\\sqrt{\\\\sum_{\\\\mu=1}^n(x^\\\\mu-x_0^\\\\mu)^2}$. Here, rather than the exponent, $x^\\\\mu$ means the $\\\\mu$-th component of $x$, this is the common agreement in differential geometry.\\n\\nIn fact, this is the direct generalization of an open interval in $\\\\mathbb{R}$.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Discrete topology]\\n\\nFor an arbitrary set $X$, we define $\\\\mathscr{T}$ to be the set containing all subsets of $X$. This is a boring topology.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Iniscrete topology]\\n\\nFor an arbitrary set $X$, we define $\\\\mathscr{T} = \\\\{\\\\varnothing, X\\\\}$. This is again a boring topology.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n# Definition: Continuity\\n\\nFor a function $f(x)$ in the usual sense (defined on $\\\\mathbb{R}$), we define its continuity using the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language: \\n\\n:::note[Continuity] \\n\\nIf $\\\\forall \\\\varepsilon > 0$, $\\\\exists \\\\delta > 0$, such that whenever $\\\\left|f(x) - f(x_0)\\\\right| < \\\\varepsilon$, $\\\\left|x - x_0\\\\right| < \\\\delta$ follows. \\n\\n:::\\n\\nHowever, currently we only have a topological space, on which there is likely no well-defined addition, nor can we define an \\"order\\", thus making it impossible to compare magnitudes. \\nAt the same time, we do not have a \\"metric\\" to measure the \\"distance\\" between two elements. Therefore, we turn to seek ways to expand the definition of continuity. \\nFor topological spaces, we only have the means to measure open sets, so we need to rephrase the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language in the language of sets: \\n\\n:::tip[Continuity: Set Language] \\n\\nIf for any open subset $U$ of the image $f[X]$ of the mapping $f$, its preimage $f^{-1}[U]$ is an open subset, then $f$ is continuous. \\n\\n:::\\n\\nAt first glance, this definition seems to make no sense at all, but we can indeed prove that it is equivalent to the $\\\\varepsilon - \\\\delta$ language on $\\\\mathbb{R}$ (in fact, it holds for any topological space equipped with a metric). \\nWe will skip the proof of this equivalence, as it is not necessary for physicists to pay too much attention on the details. However, the proof is quite simple (try using the definition of an open ball). \\n\\nFor a general function, we use $C^r$ to denote that all derivatives up to the $r$-th order exist and are continuous. In particular, $C^0$ refers to continuous functions and $C^\\\\infty$ to smooth functions. \\nWe will adopt this notation for more general mappings as well. Note that for sets with only a topological structure, the highest requirement for mappings defined on them is $C^0$. \\n\\n# Definition: Homeomorphism \\n\\nA homeomorphism is a mapping used to measure how similar two topological spaces are. Specifically, we define: \\n\\n:::tip [Homeomorphism] \\n\\nIf there exists a mapping $f$ between two topological spaces $(X, \\\\mathscr{T})$ and $(Y, \\\\mathscr{S})$ that satisfies the following conditions: \\n1. $f$ is a bijection;\\n2. Both $f$ and $f^{-1}$ are continuous mappings. \\nThen these two topological spaces are said to be homeomorphic to each other, and $f$ is a **homeomorphism** mapping. \\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note[Note] \\nGenerally speaking, if $f$ is a continuous mapping and its inverse exists, then its inverse mapping is also continuous. However, you can force a counterexample by using the discrete topology and the trivial topology mentioned earlier: \\n\\nLet the topology of $(X, \\\\mathscr{T})$ be the discrete topology (where all subsets are open subsets), and the topology of $(Y, \\\\mathscr{S})$ be the indiscrete topology (where only $Y$ and $\\\\varnothing$ are open subsets). Then, for any bijection $f$, it is continuous: only $Y = f[X]$ is an open subset, and its preimage $f^{-1}[Y] = X$ is also an open set; however, for any inverse mapping $f^{-1}$, it is not continuous: any subset $U \\\\subseteq X$ is an open subset, but its preimage $f^{-1}[U]$ is not an open subset, unless $U = X$. \\n\\nThis is almost the only application of discrete topology and coherent topology, and it is as boring as their definitions. \\n:::"}]}}')}}]);